ease with Pulmonary Involvement:\n  Favours first:  chest pain, cough, cyanosis, dyspnea, headache\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal intestine morphology, anal fissures, anemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n\nRelated to Inflammatory Bowel Disease with Pulmonary Involvement: cap polyposis, chronic granulomatous disease, colitis (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: Kartagener's syndrome: a case report. > Introduction\n  This abnormal function impairs the ability of cilia to function in a coordinated manner, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing chronic upper and lower respiratory inflammation.[5] Primary ciliar\n[RAG context for 'A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection' (score=0.71):]\n  Source: The role and importance of club cells (Clara cells) in the pathogenesis of some respiratory diseases. > Pathophysiology\n  The diagnosis of PCD, though complex and often missed or misdiagnosed, frequently involves analysis of cilia at an ultrastructural level and molecular genetic testing with one of the 33 genes associat\n[RAG context for 'The patient lives in a foster home and has been admitted many times' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: Kartagener's syndrome: a case report. > Introduction\n  This abnormal function impairs the ability of cilia to function in a coordinated manner, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing chronic upper and lower respiratory inflammation.[5] Primary ciliar\n[RAG context for 'The patient lives in a foster home and has been admitted many times' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: Kartagener's syndrome: a case report. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Most newborns with primary ciliary dysfunction (PCD), formerly known as immotile cilia syndrome, develop neonatal respiratory distress with atelectasis visible on chest radiographs. Unlike other cause\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  repeated episodes of pain/pressure over frontal sinuses and chronic mucus-producing cough since birth → congenital ciliary dysfunction syndrome → Ciliary Dysfunction Syndrome with Sinus and Pulmonary Manifestations\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for immotile cilia syndrome with nasal nitric oxide testing and ciliary biopsy to confirm the intermediate pathology\n  chronic mucus-producing cough and growth failure since birth → CFTR-related chronic pulmonary disease with pancreatic insufficiency → Classic Cystic Fibrosis with ΔF508 or Severe CFTR Mutations and Pancreatic Insufficiency\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess pancreatic function (stool elastase) and confirm with CFTR gene mutation analysis to identify the underlying genetic mechanism\n  repeated respiratory infections and abnormal growth since birth → genetic syndrome with failure to thrive and airway abnormalities → Genetic Syndromes with Cardiopulmonary and Growth Manifestations\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Perform whole exome sequencing to identify potential genetic syndromes with overlapping cardiopulmonary and growth manifestations\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
